---
import PageLayout from "../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import CardList from "../../components/CardList.astro";
import { topicDescriptions, topicLabels } from "../../lib/topics";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Modules"
  description="All LLMs Unplugged modules — the hands-on activities that lessons are built from."
>
  <h1>Modules</h1>

  <p>
    Modules are the building blocks of <em>LLMs Unplugged</em>: each one is a self-contained
    hands-on activity teaching one idea about how language models work. For a ready-to-run session
    built from them (slides, timings, materials), start from the <a href="/lessons/">lessons</a>.
    New here? Do <a href="/modules/training/">Training</a>, then
    <a href="/modules/generation/">Generation</a>, then pick whichever extensions catch your eye.
  </p>

  <p>There are three ways to run a paper model:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Grid</strong>: pen, paper and a d10. Tally word pairs into a table, then roll to
      sample (a 60/40 split is &ldquo;1&ndash;6 the first word, 7&ndash;10 the second&rdquo;). It
      plugs straight into the probability curriculum.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ledger</strong>: one row per word on a sheet, with a tally in a coloured box for each
      word that followed it. To generate, put one counter per tally mark in a cup and draw one
      without looking: the cup does the maths, so there are no dice or percentages. The
      <a href="/tools/#ledgers">Materials</a> page has ready-to-print sets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Search sheets</strong>: the whole text dealt across the room, one sheet each, and the
      room generates by show of hands. No training and no cutting: it's how we run the activity as a
      talk, or with a big class and thirty minutes. See
      <a href="/modules/search-sheets/">Search Sheets</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Training and Generation have a toggle at the top of the page for the grid, the ledger and a
    third version for younger groups, <a href="/tools/#cutouts">cutouts</a>: printed word pairs
    spread on a table and matched by colour. All three teach the same ideas.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fundamentals">Fundamentals</h2>
  <CardList topic="fundamentals" />

  <h2 id="extensions">Extensions</h2>
  <p>
    These can be done in any order after the Fundamentals. Each one explores a different aspect of
    how modern language models work.
  </p>

  <h3 id="scaling-up">{topicLabels["scaling-up"]}</h3>
  <p>{topicDescriptions["scaling-up"]}</p>
  <CardList topic="scaling-up" />

  <h3 id="extending-the-model">{topicLabels["extending-the-model"]}</h3>
  <p>{topicDescriptions["extending-the-model"]}</p>
  <CardList topic="extending-the-model" />

  <h3 id="shaping-behaviour">{topicLabels["shaping-behaviour"]}</h3>
  <p>{topicDescriptions["shaping-behaviour"]}</p>
  <CardList topic="shaping-behaviour" />

  <h3 id="looking-inside">{topicLabels["looking-inside"]}</h3>
  <p>{topicDescriptions["looking-inside"]}</p>
  <CardList topic="looking-inside" />
</PageLayout>
